# The login picture (vtium/vtium#63, ADR-1100 D17): a picture that vtium.conf
# names with login-image, which vtium places as a background beneath the text
# each time a console's session starts, before the session prints anything.
# From then on it is an ordinary picture: colored cells cover it, a screen
# clear removes it, and so does a kitty graphics delete by its depth.
#
# The guide runs in console 1 of the head, and guide.sh clears console 1's
# screen at its start and between steps, which removes the picture there.
# So each step that judges the picture opens a new console with
# Ctrl+Super+Enter, where vtium places the picture again before that
# console's shell starts. In that console guide.sh runs login-console (the
# console: line below), which lists the commands the steps ask for, so the
# operator reads them there, and starts a shell with the prompt console-N$.
#
# The run needs a build with images. run-vt.sh gives vtium the configuration
# beside this guide, login.conf, in place of the review's: it names the Arch
# Linux logo, a blue arch on a transparent background, with
# login-image-fit=cover. Edit login.conf to try another picture. The banner
# form, login-image-fit=banner, needs a configuration of its own, and this
# guide does not check it.
#
#   make vt-check GUIDE=login FEATURES=images

title: the login picture behind a new console's prompt

intro: vtium places the login picture each time a console's session starts,
intro: before anything prints. This guide runs in console 1 and clears the
intro: screen for each step, which removes the picture here. So the steps
intro: open new consoles with Ctrl+Super+Enter and look at the picture there.
intro: Ctrl+Super+Left and Ctrl+Super+Right switch between the consoles.

console: login-console

step: The configuration names the picture
run: c=/run/vtium-vt/config/vtium.conf; grep '^login-image' "$c" && ls -l -- "$(sed -n 's/^login-image=//p' "$c")"
expect: two lines from vtium's configuration: login-image= with the path of
expect: a picture file, and login-image-fit=cover. Then ls lists that file,
expect: owned by root. *If a line or the file is missing, answer n*: the
expect: steps after this one then show no picture.

step: The picture behind a new console's prompt
do: here in console 1, press Ctrl+Super+Enter. vtium opens console 2 and
do: shows console 2. Look at the whole screen of console 2 before you type
do: anything. Then press Ctrl+Super+Left to come back to console 1, and
do: leave console 2 open.
expect: in console 2, the picture, the blue arch of the Arch Linux emblem,
expect: *fills the screen* behind the text. It is scaled up until it is as
expect: wide as the screen, keeping its shape, so its top and bottom are cut
expect: off. Where the picture is transparent, the default background shows. The lines at the top,
expect: the list of commands and the prompt console-2$ read cleanly.

step: Colored cells cover the picture
do: here in console 1, press Ctrl+Super+Right to show console 2. At the
do: prompt console-2$, type cells and press Enter. Look at the three lines
do: that cells prints in the middle of the screen, over the picture. Then
do: press Ctrl+Super+Left to come back to console 1.
expect: in console 2, the red cells and the reversed cells are *solid*, with
expect: no picture showing through them. The plain text between them draws
expect: over the picture, which shows around its letters.

step: A screen clear removes the picture
do: here in console 1, press Ctrl+Super+Right to show console 2. At the
do: prompt console-2$, type clear and press Enter, then look at the whole
do: screen. Then press Ctrl+Super+Backspace, which closes console 2 and
do: shows console 1 again.
expect: in console 2, the picture is *gone*, with the default background
expect: everywhere, and the prompt console-2$ at the top. The red cells and
expect: the reversed cells are gone too.

step: Another new console gets its own picture
do: here in console 1, press Ctrl+Super+Enter. vtium opens console 3 and
do: shows console 3. Look at the whole screen of console 3. Then press
do: Ctrl+Super+Left to come back to console 1, and leave console 3 open.
expect: console 3 shows the picture behind its text *at once*, as console 2
expect: did before the clear.

step: A delete by image id leaves the picture
do: here in console 1, press Ctrl+Super+Right to show console 3. At the
do: prompt console-3$, type delete-id and press Enter, then look at the
do: whole screen. Then press Ctrl+Super+Left to come back to console 1.
expect: in console 3, the picture *stays* as it was. The login picture has no
expect: image id, so a delete of image id 1 does not reach it. No reply
expect: shows at the prompt.

step: A delete by depth removes the picture
do: here in console 1, press Ctrl+Super+Right to show console 3. At the
do: prompt console-3$, type delete-depth and press Enter, then look at the
do: whole screen. Then press Ctrl+Super+Backspace, which closes console 3
do: and shows console 1 again.
expect: in console 3, the picture is *gone*, and the text stays. A delete of
expect: every picture at depth -2147483648, the lowest depth there is,
expect: removes the login picture alone.
